@ NPSGOV-HC-VIRGINIA-MONUMENT
1 | monuments | Virginia Monument: the Virginia Gettysburg Commission selected Spangler's Woods as the site in December 1909 | - | !1909-12 (site chosen for the Virginia memorial; erected after the battle) | stated | The finding aid says a meeting of the Virginia Gettysburg Commission in Washington selected a site for a Virginia memorial on the Gettysburg battlefield at Spangler's Woods and asked the Secretary of War to be advised of his approval, in a letter dated December 7, 1909 | A meeting of the Virginia Gettysburg Commission met in Washington D.C. and selected a site for a Virginia memorial to be erected on the Gettysburg Battlefield at "Spangler's Woods" and ask Secretary of War, Henry Stimson "may be advised of your approval of our action." | 3b. Thomas Smith, Secretary, Virginia Gettysburg Commission (VGC) to Secretary of War, Henry L. Stimson, December 7, 1909
1 | monuments | Virginia Monument: the Governor planned the dedication for June 1 or 2, 1917 | - | !1917-06 (planned dedication of the Virginia Monument; erected after the battle) | stated | The finding aid says the Virginia Governor's letter of November 17, 1916 plans the dedication for June 1 or 2, 1917, after the Confederate Reunion in Washington on May 28 and 29, 1917 | The Dedication will be scheduled for June 1st or 2 nd 1917, to follow the Confederate Reunion in Washington DC scheduled for May 28 th and 29 th , 1917. | Governor of Virginia, H.C. Stuart to John P. Nicolson, Chairman, GNPC, November 17, 1916
1 | monuments | Virginia Monument: a letter of June 12, 1917 thanks the commission for the Virginia dedication, which had drawn fewer people from the Washington reunion than expected | - | !1917-06 (the dedication of the Virginia Monument was over by June 12, 1917) | stated | The finding aid says that in his letter of June 12, 1917 Mr. Van Amringe thanks Mr. Nicolson for the Virginia Dedication and mentions that it was expected to draw many participants from the Confederate Reunion in Washington held a few days earlier, but did not | He also mentions that the Virginia Dedication was expected to draw many of the participants from the Confederate Reunion in Washington held a few days earlier, but it did not. | 34. W.B. Van Amringe, The Van-Amringe Granite Co., to Lt Col John P. Nicolson, Chairman, GNPC, June 12, 1917
